Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||06-03-2001||01:36 AM||||You have mail!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierokid87||06-03-2001||10:03 AM||||Hey Joe.  hehe  Nice looking Fiero.  looks alot like mine used to look when I first got it.  Scince then I have added the wing, the GT lacey wheels, and a SUNROOF.  Ill take pictures of my install when I go down to work more on my Fiero (V-6 conversion).  I put you on my AOL Insant measanger list.  so we can talk that way.  I have the instructions on my web site.  <A HREF="http://www.anglefire.com/sports/sfiero" TARGET=_blank>http://www.anglefire.com/sports/sfiero</A>   So there you go have fun, good luck and talk to you soon.<P>Scott<P>AIM:  Fierokid87<BR>ICQ:  Fierokid||||reg||4
Z||000004||Fierokid87||06-03-2001||10:05 AM||||Ooops check that web site.  its <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/sports/sfiero/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/sports/sfiero/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Master_Sushi||06-03-2001||12:27 PM||||Im in the middle of a sunroof swap. I have an 84se that im taking the roof out of. The bigest question I have is about the headliner. Will the headliner out of the 84 work in my 88 coupe? Also it needs to be recovered, where can I get the cloth for it?<P>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000006||mbramble||06-03-2001||12:36 PM||||Headliners should fit any year car.  <P>You can purchase headliner material at a local auto upholstery shop.  I bought enough material for two headliners and a can of the spray on adhesive that the shop recommended for $16.||||reg||1
Z||000007||84Bill||06-03-2001||12:43 PM||||Just spen several hours removing the roof from my donor.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/sunroof/image002.jpg">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/sunroof/image004.jpg">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/sunroof/image006.jpg">  <P>Be prepared to spend a lot of time getting it out. My first attempt (several years ago) resulted in a multipiece unit.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Oh and here is some of the items you asked to be shown<BR> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/sunroof/image018an.jpg">  <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-03-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000008||Devotshka||06-03-2001||12:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Master_Sushi:<BR><B>Will the headliner out of the 84 work in my 88 coupe? <BR>Thanks</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You'll need to cut out the provision for the 88 coupe's third brake light, otherwise it will fit fine.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Master_Sushi||06-03-2001||12:59 PM||||Ok i have one more question. When I took the roof off the doner car I broke it in one place  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">. If I epoxy this together will a shop be able to make it look good again?<P>thanks||||reg||5
Z||000010||87GTjoe||06-03-2001||02:06 PM||||Dont forget...There are also two rubber pads that mount on the inside of the hood.They keep the glass from moving around during storage.||||reg||1
Z||000011||FieroJoe||06-03-2001||05:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Master_Sushi:<BR><B>Ok i have one more question. When I took the roof off the doner car I broke it in one place   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">. If I epoxy this together will a shop be able to make it look good again?<P>thanks</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I wouldnt reccomend epoxy.  Go down to your local hardware or body shop supply and get a Bondo Fiberglass Repair kit.  It comes with fiberglass cloth, resin, hardener, and everything you'll need.  You'll lay the fiberglass cloth on the backside of your crack, and use the resin to hold it all together.  Simple instructions are included.<P>Thanks all for the pics, I see something new here that I havent heard of before.  The deflector, is that just a long strip of plastic there?  where does it/how does it go on?  And the sunshade, doesnt that clip into the underside of the hood?||||reg||1
Z||000012||mckay_lee||06-03-2001||08:28 PM||||I have the sunroof glass if anybody is interested i would like to sell it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||84Bill||06-04-2001||12:45 AM||||The deflector has tabs on it that clip in the slots on the roof. It is basicly a strip of formed plastic.||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||06-04-2001||11:57 AM||||I don't see how anyone can stand a car this small w/o the sun roof. Mine is open year round. It's out about every chance I get...<P>Depends just how it broke but as long as you're careful the break will become nearly invisible if you epoxy it. The most you'll have to do is sand off a little epoxy that squeezes out. (Try not to smear it around the front side of the work...) If it needs any filler in chiped areas it should be minimal. "Spot" putty is probably all you'd need.<P>I usually like the plastic welder epoxy for this stuff. Seems to get a little better bite than regular epoxies on the resins. If it's in an area where you think it might be weak and crack again, you can stregnthen it with a little glass mat on the back side. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||FieroJoe||06-04-2001||07:33 PM||||84Bill : Have you tried sitting your sunroof glass in the front compartment?  I think I remember someone saying the washer fluid bottle's have to be swapped to the smalled kind due to the clearances.<P>BTW: Went to the junkyard today.  They said they had 2 or 3, but one might have been moved (crushed?)  So I get to the two that are there, and one has an aftermarket sunroof (POS install), and the other a hardtop.  But, laying next to the front fascia of a white 84 2m4 is a sunroof glass panel, so the one that must have been moved probably had the sunroof  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I didnt pick up the glass because it was in pretty crappy condition, the tint was all wearing away.||||reg||1
Z||000016||84Bill||06-04-2001||09:41 PM||||I have already done a sunroof swap on my 88 and there was no problem with the washer fluid bottle clearence. I have not done the swap on my 84 yet. My Indy and 84SE both have the same washer bottle so I think its safe to say there is not a clearence problem with the 84's as well. However I will test fit the parts and take a few pics just the same  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000017||84Bill||06-05-2001||06:07 PM||||Here is a picture of the roof in the trunk of my 84SE that did not come with a sunroof<P> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/sunroof1/image008.jpg"> <P>and grab this part too just be carefull removing it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/sunroof1/image010.jpg"> <P>here is where it goes on the hood<P> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/sunroof1/image012.jpg"> <BR>||||reg||14
Z||000018||FieroJoe||06-05-2001||10:04 PM||||whoa cool, thanks for the pics, that glass fits just perfectly almost!<P>A question about the black thingy (shield? one with the honeycomb vents) that you have to take off to get at the lower part of the roof panel.  Does it completely have to come off, or can you just peel it back enough to get at the bolts that are holding the panel on at the bottom.  I noticed that my hood spring is rivited down, and would be the last thing holding it completely on.  Seems to me that I should be able to get a box end wrench down there, but I dont know what angle they'd be at.<P>Anything else I should know about before I go back to a different junkyard (called today, said they had 4 Fieros, didnt know anything else about them though)?  I've got a coping saw blade (still looking for old guitar string), my socket set and a bunch of screwdrivers.||||reg||1
Z||000019||84Bill||06-06-2001||07:49 AM||||The sunroof rest must be adjusted just a bit for the glass to fit better. I'll take a rubber mallet to the 3 mounts to get that rest to move backjust a tiny bit<P>Thats the Cowl cover. And yes it must be removed by taking out the phillips head screws that secure it. The spring is attached to the cowl cover and not the frame of the car      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Other things you should know about removing the roof! <BR>It's the last part of the car you can remove. Basicly you must remove interior headliner and post pieces and seatbelt anchors, fenders must come off. <P>Lets put it this way everything must come off the car except the hood door pannels lower quarters, rear clip and trunkdeck. it will take an hour. before you can even get to hacking at the roof panel<P>FYI<BR>when I removed the roof panel I found it easier to remove the windshield trim and draw a utility knife around the windshield. then I used a prybar to lightly lift the roof panel enough to get a steak knife under there to cut the goop. So I would just Lift it up cut a few inches stop move the prybar, lift the roof and cut a few more inches.<BR>Now I busted up the windshield by prying against it but I didn't care.<P>oops, almost had you removing the rear clip   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000020||FieroJoe||06-06-2001||09:16 AM||||I have tried to remove my fenders before, I could get them to bend back quite a bit.  I didnt figure out how to get it out of the rocker sill where it is attached at the bottom, so I didnt bother any more.  I am not worried about breaking anything on the car Im taking the panel from, but when I go to put it in my car, should I be able to get at the bolts just by bending the fender back enough?<P>I guess when I tried to take the cowl off my car I didnt pull hard enough on that rivet.||||reg||1
Z||000021||84Bill||06-06-2001||02:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJoe:<BR><B>should I be able to get at the bolts just by bending the fender back enough?<P>I guess when I tried to take the cowl off my car I didnt pull hard enough on that rivet.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Yeah bending the fenders back should do it. <BR>The cowl spring is rivited to the frame? humm interesting it's not on my 88 but then again I had the windshield replaced a few times, maybe they got smart and just rivited it to the cowl panel and not the frame.<P>Just bang out the center of the rivit with a punch or small phillips head screw driver then get a long flathead and shove it between the cowl and the pannel and whack it a few times with a hammer.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000022||FieroJoe||06-07-2001||06:33 PM||||Well I got most of my parts today.  Removing the panel was not that hard at all.  I busted up the windshield though, that made it easier.  I just bashed it in with a hammer, and then sawed it away and folded it into the interior.  I did bring a guitar string, but managed to get it loose with a screwdriver and a utility knife.<P>I pretty got everything that I need, a few extras too.  I've got a 2 wind deflectors, and 2 latch assemblys.  Problem is though, I don't have any glass!  The one that must have been on this one had to have been busted up, all that was left was the hinge and the little tabs with some glass still left around the bolts.  So, anybody got a peice of sunroof glass they can sell me?  I also have a topic in the Mall.<P>Anyway, here are some pics:<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sunroof1.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sunroof2.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000023||84Bill||06-07-2001||07:50 PM||||excelent Job!<BR>I like the lift and cut method better than hacking away with a guitar string. hummm I could use one of those deflectors.<P>Got this in an email today regarding sunroof glass if you want it I'll point this guy your way.<P>"how does $50 plus shipping sound, i have no idea what shipping would be though.<BR>It does have a few scratches, but i am told that you can buy something that will<BR>get them out. Ohter than that the sunroof is in good condition."<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000024||FieroJoe||06-07-2001||09:57 PM||||84Bill - I was going to email you, but no adress given!  Send me an email and we can talk about the deflector...||||reg||1
Z||000025||Master_Sushi||06-07-2001||11:04 PM||||This has been very helpfull to me also. I almost wish it came up a week or two ago before I started this project. But thanks for all the great info. I did use epoxy to fix my broken roof and its holding very well <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. I did pretty much destroy the windshield on the 84 doner while removing the roof, which is a *itch because I do plan on restoring it.<P>I only have two things im not sure of now. All  the black goop that holds the roof on. Will I have to replace it or make extra sure that its in the right place? Is it a sealer or just an adheasive? Oh, and also Ive heard about 84 sunroofs not haveing drain holes or something. Will this be a problum since im installing it into an 88?<P>Thanks for the help, cant wait to cruse with the top off  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"> .||||reg||14
Z||000026||FieroJoe||06-08-2001||07:34 AM||||Yes, I do believe that you'll have to drill some drain holes, but I am not sure where they need to go.  If someone can post a pic, that'll help you and me both.<P>As for the goop, I believe that it is butyl rubber.  You should be able to get that at a auto glass shop, it probably comes in a roll.  You should replace it.||||reg||1
Z||000027||84Bill||06-08-2001||08:21 AM||||Sunroof holes you ask?<BR>Here is a thread with pics <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010923.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010923.html</A> <P>Fierojoe<BR>My email should be working   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>wh_stewart@hotmail.com<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000028||FieroJoe||06-08-2001||09:25 AM||||Email sent!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>You don't have an email button in your posts and there isnt an adress in your profile.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Ship Rider||06-10-2001||09:22 PM||||I had to lift my fiberglass roof a bit and think I broke the "goop" seal.  Is it critical that this seal be intact to avoid a leak?  Do I need to try and reseal it and is there any suggestion on doing it without having to remove the roof?  I have the car apart to paint so the aft fairing is off.  I noticed the headliner was wet after a heavy rain, but am not sure if it is coming from the sunroof seal or the broken "Goop" seal in the subroof.  I sure hope I don't have to pull the roof to fix the seal??? By replacing the rear fairing, will I reduce the amount of water that gets to the subroof enough to keeit from leaking?  Thanks in advance for any advice.||||reg||5
Z||000030||84Bill||06-11-2001||07:27 AM||||That all depends on where it's getting wet. Fornt, rear, side? I'm sure most will agree the sunroof usually leaks in the rear.||||reg||1
Z||000031||FieroJoe||06-11-2001||07:37 AM||||And, correct me if I am wrong, but from what I've been reading, arent most sunroof leaks actually caused by the seals around the bolt holes wearing away?  By the looks of the hardware I got, they're pretty well rotten, so I'll replace them with some rubber washers, and probably a little silicone caulk inside the hole itself.||||reg||1
Z||000032||84Bill||06-11-2001||12:10 PM||||Some leak from the latch mounts. All of my leaks have been gasket related. I always replace the rubber seals in the glass so thats never been a factor for me||||reg||1
Z||000033||Carrolles||06-12-2001||08:28 AM||||FieroJoe,<P>I bought all the mounting hardware from the local Pontiac dealer for under $20 US.  This included the new bolts/nuts with the integral washers, new mounting tabs (the part that stabs into the roof panel), and latch spacer.<P>These fixed my intermittant roof leaks.||||reg||1
